00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:18.920
There's a lot of
takeaways here that we'll

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:23.120
talk about that really touch the
cornerstone of enterprise use

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:26.240
cases but also can be applied
across different segments

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:27.920
of cloud consumption.

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:30.410
So what do most of our
enterprise customers

00:02:30.410 --> 00:02:32.420
want as they move to the cloud?

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:34.460
Traditionally, a lot
of the conversations

00:02:34.460 --> 00:02:36.460
that we're having with
customers a few years ago

00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:40.400
were around data center
consolidation and cost savings.

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:42.728
That conversation has
shifted somewhat from,

00:02:42.728 --> 00:02:44.020
why should I move to the cloud?

00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:45.353
Is it safe to move to the cloud?

00:02:45.353 --> 00:02:48.980
To how can I move to the
cloud as fast as possible

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:50.930
and how can you help me?

00:02:50.930 --> 00:02:54.200
The idea here is we want
to introduce quick wins,

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:57.110
so that we can actually
pull away lessons and start

00:02:57.110 --> 00:03:00.210
to actually change our behavior
earlier on in the process.

00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:02.840
This allows us to actually
drive digital innovation,

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:05.090
and we can actually
maximize our velocity

00:03:05.090 --> 00:03:06.810
to then move to the cloud.

00:03:06.810 --> 00:03:08.750
So we want to move really quick.

00:03:08.750 --> 00:03:12.222
We want to be able to not repeat
bad behaviors in the cloud.

00:03:12.222 --> 00:03:14.180
But by doing that, we
need to do that in a very

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:15.290
secure and compliant way.

00:03:15.290 --> 00:03:17.750
So repeatability control
is something we definitely

00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:19.457
see from our customers.

00:03:19.457 --> 00:03:21.290
All of our customers
want to be able to move

00:03:21.290 --> 00:03:23.750
in a secure and compliant
way and build somewhat

00:03:23.750 --> 00:03:26.330
of an abstract
approach to how they

00:03:26.330 --> 00:03:28.820
work across multiple
environments in the cloud.

00:03:28.820 --> 00:03:30.920
By abstract, we mean
deploying services

00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:35.750
across multiple cloud platforms
in a very predictable way.

00:03:35.750 --> 00:03:40.740
This lends developers to focus
on higher level types of work.

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:42.740
And then we can actually
reduce the blast radius

00:03:42.740 --> 00:03:43.830
for when things go wrong.

00:03:43.830 --> 00:03:45.495
So sometimes things
happen to go wrong,

00:03:45.495 --> 00:03:47.120
and we want to be
able to minimize that

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:50.180
as much as possible with
having the right guardrails

00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:53.480
and controls in place on your
foundations and infrastructure.

00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:55.340
So I touched on this
in the beginning.

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:58.160
So cost savings is
very much a key driver

00:03:58.160 --> 00:03:59.460
to move to the cloud.

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:01.700
But it's somewhat become
part of your point of entry

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:03.240
to move to the public cloud.

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:05.120
So at this point, we're
seeing our customers

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:07.580
really wanting to get
deeper understanding

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:10.700
of their operations to
understand what their behaviors

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:13.340
and functions are in the
cloud so they can build

00:04:13.340 --> 00:04:15.770
high fidelity data models
to track that and change

00:04:15.770 --> 00:04:16.682
those behaviors.

00:04:16.682 --> 00:04:18.140
So the idea is that
we can actually

00:04:18.140 --> 00:04:21.240
start to realize
deeper cost savings

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:24.110
and then start to improve and
mature our infrastructure so

00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:27.430
that we can save more money.

00:04:27.430 --> 00:04:29.380
So how do we get there?

00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:33.085
What are the typical things that
we see in the enterprise space

00:04:33.085 --> 00:04:35.460
where we can get to a place
where we are fully automated,

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:37.182
we have infrastructures,
code pipeline,

00:04:37.182 --> 00:04:38.640
and we're starting
to actually take

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:41.730
those key aspects of
our usage of the cloud

00:04:41.730 --> 00:04:44.675
and then change our behavior
to get more cost savings?

00:04:44.675 --> 00:04:46.050
As simple as it
sounds, it starts

00:04:46.050 --> 00:04:48.390
with actually defining
a set of best practices

00:04:48.390 --> 00:04:51.660
that your organization
sees the best fit for you.

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:53.910
The idea here is to
actually take key aspects,

00:04:53.910 --> 00:04:57.270
such as how you authenticate to
the cloud, your identity access

00:04:57.270 --> 00:05:02.130
management, your security
posture, your networking,

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:04.320
your logging and monitoring,
as well as your billing

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:05.435
foundations.

00:05:05.435 --> 00:05:07.560
Our professional services
team works really closely

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:09.990
with customers in the enterprise
space to help them step

00:05:09.990 --> 00:05:13.890
through getting started
with their best practices.

00:05:13.890 --> 00:05:15.900
The idea here is to
actually include billing

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:18.180
as part of your foundation
scope so that you

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:21.187
have the folks in the
room early on that

00:05:21.187 --> 00:05:23.020
are making key decisions
that are ultimately

00:05:23.020 --> 00:05:25.230
responsible for your
spend in the cloud.

00:05:25.230 --> 00:05:28.440
The idea here is to actually
go through a workshop

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:31.140
to understand what your labeling
schema should look like,

00:05:31.140 --> 00:05:33.120
make sure that
procurement folks that

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:35.490
are building the chargeback
for the public cloud

00:05:35.490 --> 00:05:37.888
have a say as far as what
the key and value pairs are

00:05:37.888 --> 00:05:39.930
that you're actually
putting on top of your cloud

00:05:39.930 --> 00:05:42.330
infrastructure.

00:05:42.330 --> 00:05:45.000
Then we move into actually
defining your GCP resource

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:45.635
hierarchy.

00:05:45.635 --> 00:05:47.010
This is another
aspect of getting

00:05:47.010 --> 00:05:49.200
to a place where you
can start understanding

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:51.290
your consumption in the cloud.

00:05:51.290 --> 00:05:54.420
Your GCP resource hierarchy is
essentially your organization

00:05:54.420 --> 00:05:56.100
onto GCP.

00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:58.400
And there's three key
components there--

00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:01.090
organizations,
folders, and projects.

00:06:01.090 --> 00:06:04.560
And what this allows you to do
is group all your GCP resources

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:07.230
in a way that you can query,
filter, and then change

00:06:07.230 --> 00:06:09.090
your behavior against that.

00:06:09.090 --> 00:06:12.355
The idea here is that when we
work with a lot of customers,

00:06:12.355 --> 00:06:14.730
we'd go through an exercise
to understand how we actually

00:06:14.730 --> 00:06:16.530
break that folder structure out.

00:06:16.530 --> 00:06:19.050
A lot of our customers
will break out their folder

00:06:19.050 --> 00:06:21.550
structure based on
some development cycle,

00:06:21.550 --> 00:06:24.480
whether that be a
QA, dev, production,

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:26.910
a common shared folders project.

00:06:26.910 --> 00:06:30.000
And the idea is that we can
then take this folder structure

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:31.890
and then build a common
project deployment

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:35.080
framework against that folder.

00:06:35.080 --> 00:06:37.530
There's actually a session
later today at 2:10

00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:40.340
PM called CST200 that'll
actually step you through some

00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:42.090
of the best practices
and how you actually

00:06:42.090 --> 00:06:45.480
get started with getting set
up with your GCP resource

00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:47.540
hierarchy.

00:06:47.540 --> 00:06:49.682
We want to be able to focus
on repeatable patterns.

00:06:49.682 --> 00:06:51.890
So we want to understand
what users and operators are

00:06:51.890 --> 00:06:54.140
going to do most in the cloud.

00:06:54.140 --> 00:06:55.820
I'll talk about
this in a bit here,

00:06:55.820 --> 00:06:58.250
but project creation
is an excellent example

00:06:58.250 --> 00:07:02.390
of how you can actually start to
introduce a repeatable pattern

00:07:02.390 --> 00:07:04.850
and then build a lot of the
best practices into that.

00:07:04.850 --> 00:07:07.400
So we can start building
reusable declarative

00:07:07.400 --> 00:07:12.050
templates that allow us to scale
that foundation deployment.

00:07:12.050 --> 00:07:15.600
Selecting a tool-- so Google
wants you, as a customer,

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:18.170
to select the right tool that
works for your organization.

00:07:18.170 --> 00:07:19.850
There's a few key
considerations there.

00:07:19.850 --> 00:07:23.480
One is around the
training and skill sets

00:07:23.480 --> 00:07:24.590
available within your org.

00:07:24.590 --> 00:07:26.493
So we encourage
you to really align

00:07:26.493 --> 00:07:28.910
the vision and direction you
want to take your developers'

00:07:28.910 --> 00:07:29.883
skills.

00:07:29.883 --> 00:07:31.050
There's a few options there.

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:34.440
One is a native Google solution
called Deployment Manager

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:37.183
that's fully API supportable.

00:07:37.183 --> 00:07:38.850
With Google's commitment
to open source,

00:07:38.850 --> 00:07:41.150
there's also another tool
called Terraform, which

00:07:41.150 --> 00:07:42.708
I'll talk about here shortly.

00:07:42.708 --> 00:07:44.750
That also allows you to
build your infrastructure

00:07:44.750 --> 00:07:48.230
as code pipeline and take
some of these common patterns

00:07:48.230 --> 00:07:51.460
and deploy them widely.

00:07:51.460 --> 00:07:54.340
Now, we have our best practices.

00:07:54.340 --> 00:07:56.148
We know our GCP
resource hierarchy.

00:07:56.148 --> 00:07:57.940
We have an understanding
of the foundations

00:07:57.940 --> 00:07:59.200
and common patterns.

00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:00.700
We've selected a tool.

00:08:00.700 --> 00:08:04.010
Now we want to get into a place
we have extreme automation.

00:08:04.010 --> 00:08:06.070
So the idea of this
is to basically codify

00:08:06.070 --> 00:08:08.320
all of our best
practices in a way that

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:11.620
allows us to scale them and
remove any single person that's

00:08:11.620 --> 00:08:14.320
doing manual deployments
across the environment.

00:08:14.320 --> 00:08:17.170
We want to be able to take
elements of projects, networks,

00:08:17.170 --> 00:08:19.420
identity access
management, and security,

00:08:19.420 --> 00:08:22.240
codify them, and then also
treat them just as dynamic

00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:25.560
as our application code.

00:08:25.560 --> 00:08:29.100
Terraform-- so just going to
give a quick background as far

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:31.450
as what Terraform
is in this context.

00:08:31.450 --> 00:08:34.590
So Terraform is an open source
infrastructure as code tool

00:08:34.590 --> 00:08:36.070
by HashiCorp.

00:08:36.070 --> 00:08:38.320
Terraform has this
concept of providers.

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:41.130
So it allows you to interact
with various public cloud

00:08:41.130 --> 00:08:45.270
sources, such as GCP, AWS, and
Azure, as well as higher level

00:08:45.270 --> 00:08:48.690
infrastructure services,
like Kubernetes.

00:08:48.690 --> 00:08:51.840
Terraform declaratively defines
in a set of configuration files

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:54.120
what you want your
infrastructure to look like.

00:08:54.120 --> 00:08:57.810
It'll give you a topology
that you can evolve over time

00:08:57.810 --> 00:09:01.160
and build a workflow around
manning that infrastructure.

00:09:01.160 --> 00:09:03.710
Terraform works
in three key ways.

00:09:03.710 --> 00:09:07.100
There's a refresh, a
plan, and an apply.

00:09:07.100 --> 00:09:13.340
And essentially that refresh is
your infrastructure described.

00:09:13.340 --> 00:09:16.460
The plan is that real world
described in the infrastructure

00:09:16.460 --> 00:09:18.170
in a configuration file.

00:09:18.170 --> 00:09:19.730
And apply-- which
actually builds

00:09:19.730 --> 00:09:21.620
that infrastructure for you.

00:09:21.620 --> 00:09:23.750
So we take those three
elements, and then we

00:09:23.750 --> 00:09:25.475
build a module in Terraform.

00:09:25.475 --> 00:09:28.580
And a module is somewhat of a
black box of infrastructure,

00:09:28.580 --> 00:09:31.220
where you can declaratively
define the variables and input

00:09:31.220 --> 00:09:33.890
and control the
behavior of that module.

00:09:33.890 --> 00:09:35.570
This allows us to
kind of encapsulate

00:09:35.570 --> 00:09:37.490
a lot of complexity
around managing

00:09:37.490 --> 00:09:39.512
traditional infrastructure.

00:09:39.512 --> 00:09:41.470
We can take those modules
and then publish them

00:09:41.470 --> 00:09:43.300
to a centralized repository.

00:09:43.300 --> 00:09:46.450
So we'll talk about how we've
structured that in CoreLogic.

00:09:46.450 --> 00:09:50.320
But ultimately, Terraform is
an excellent open source tool.

00:09:50.320 --> 00:09:52.540
I mentioned there's
a provider for GCP.

00:09:52.540 --> 00:09:54.760
So there's thousands of
contributors publishing best

00:09:54.760 --> 00:09:56.730
practices for Terraform on GCP.

00:09:59.720 --> 00:10:02.372
Now, coming to the
project factory--

00:10:02.372 --> 00:10:04.580
I'm sure many of you are
aware of the concept of what

00:10:04.580 --> 00:10:08.360
a project is, but a GCP project
provides a trust boundary

00:10:08.360 --> 00:10:11.910
and resource isolation for
all of your GCP resources.

00:10:11.910 --> 00:10:14.990
It's a basic resource
container for policies,

00:10:14.990 --> 00:10:17.090
and infrastructure, and GCP.

00:10:17.090 --> 00:10:19.940
So we want to be able to take
a lot of our best practices--

00:10:19.940 --> 00:10:22.610
and because the project creation
is such an important part

00:10:22.610 --> 00:10:26.087
of how you control your
environment in GCP--

00:10:26.087 --> 00:10:28.670
we want to be able to take all
those best practices and harden

00:10:28.670 --> 00:10:30.840
them into the creation process.

00:10:30.840 --> 00:10:33.650
This allows us to stamp out
projects in a very consistent,

00:10:33.650 --> 00:10:35.285
compliant way.

00:10:35.285 --> 00:10:36.660
As you see here,
there's a number

00:10:36.660 --> 00:10:38.580
of things that happen
in this project factory.

00:10:38.580 --> 00:10:41.550
Everything from defining a
project in a standard naming

00:10:41.550 --> 00:10:44.580
convention, to attaching
it to a shared network,

00:10:44.580 --> 00:10:47.382
deleting all the
default configurations.

00:10:47.382 --> 00:10:48.840
It allows us to
actually go through

00:10:48.840 --> 00:10:51.820
and associate G Suite
accounts to that project.

00:10:51.820 --> 00:10:54.780
But coming down a bit, it
allows us to actually maintain

00:10:54.780 --> 00:10:57.450
a consistent framework for
how we associate projects

00:10:57.450 --> 00:11:00.090
to billing functions.

00:11:00.090 --> 00:11:02.143
Associating projects
to billing accounts--

00:11:02.143 --> 00:11:03.810
something we definitely
see in the field

00:11:03.810 --> 00:11:07.500
is that an inconsistent
project build process really

00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:10.775
prevents us from making sure
that we associate projects

00:11:10.775 --> 00:11:12.400
to billing accounts
in an accurate way.

00:11:12.400 --> 00:11:14.940
So we can accurately
describe what labels

00:11:14.940 --> 00:11:17.220
and what billing counts
we want to have associated

00:11:17.220 --> 00:11:19.140
when the project gets created.

00:11:19.140 --> 00:11:20.468
Setting up usage exports--

00:11:20.468 --> 00:11:22.260
this is just kind of
another function here.

00:11:22.260 --> 00:11:24.630
We want to be able to
enable as much metadata

00:11:24.630 --> 00:11:26.400
about our usage in GCP.

00:11:26.400 --> 00:11:29.070
So we suggest enabling
the GCE usage report

00:11:29.070 --> 00:11:32.440
and dumping that into
a central GCS bucket.

00:11:32.440 --> 00:11:38.110
Coming into applying billing
labels-- so one of the workshop

00:11:38.110 --> 00:11:39.730
items I mentioned
earlier on defining

00:11:39.730 --> 00:11:41.950
best practices is
really around kind

00:11:41.950 --> 00:11:43.240
of building that label schema.

00:11:43.240 --> 00:11:45.935
And what we want to do is
actually take the set of labels

00:11:45.935 --> 00:11:47.560
and figure out which
ones are mandatory

00:11:47.560 --> 00:11:50.560
and which ones are
optional, and make sure

00:11:50.560 --> 00:11:52.700
that we incorporate that
as part of the foundation

00:11:52.700 --> 00:11:55.863
build process for projects.

00:11:55.863 --> 00:11:58.030
With that, I'm actually
going to hand it over to Jim

00:11:58.030 --> 00:11:59.182
to talk about CoreLogic.

00:11:59.182 --> 00:12:00.390
And then we'll go from there.

00:12:03.080 --> 00:12:05.560
JIM WOLCOTT: Thank you, Ramsey.

00:12:05.560 --> 00:12:09.060
OK, so a little bit
about CoreLogic.

00:12:09.060 --> 00:12:16.540
CoreLogic helps you to find,
acquire, and protect your home.

00:12:16.540 --> 00:12:18.970
Some of the services
that we have in CoreLogic

00:12:18.970 --> 00:12:23.380
are like we provide listing
services to real estate agents

00:12:23.380 --> 00:12:27.160
and brokers, we provide
valuation information

00:12:27.160 --> 00:12:31.330
to lenders, and we
provide risk information

00:12:31.330 --> 00:12:33.520
to insurance companies.

00:12:33.520 --> 00:12:36.080
We're a global company.

00:12:36.080 --> 00:12:38.900
And we're on a
three-year mission

00:12:38.900 --> 00:12:41.300
to migrate all of our
applications and products

00:12:41.300 --> 00:12:45.010
to the Google Cloud Platform.

00:12:45.010 --> 00:12:47.860
So we've been working with
a professional services

00:12:47.860 --> 00:12:50.110
organization in that endeavor.

00:12:50.110 --> 00:12:52.510
And they've been fantastic,
especially as we've

00:12:52.510 --> 00:12:54.910
been pushing the
envelope in some areas

00:12:54.910 --> 00:12:57.490
and engage with their
engineering teams and product

00:12:57.490 --> 00:12:58.720
managers.

00:12:58.720 --> 00:13:02.740
Ramsey asked me to post
a couple of bullets

00:13:02.740 --> 00:13:05.530
about why we chose the
Google Cloud Platform.

00:13:05.530 --> 00:13:10.230
And CoreLogic, at its
heart, is a data company.

00:13:10.230 --> 00:13:13.020
We have an enormous
amount of property data

00:13:13.020 --> 00:13:16.350
in detail, which
we leverage in all

00:13:16.350 --> 00:13:18.540
of our products and services.

00:13:18.540 --> 00:13:22.480
The Google technologies are
very synergistic with this.

00:13:22.480 --> 00:13:28.030
And so it's a perfect match for
CoreLogic from that standpoint.

00:13:28.030 --> 00:13:30.570
The other thing is the
Google Cloud Platform

00:13:30.570 --> 00:13:33.170
is really easy to automate.

00:13:33.170 --> 00:13:35.520
And I know there's
a number of CSPs

00:13:35.520 --> 00:13:37.540
out there that you can
apply automation to.

00:13:37.540 --> 00:13:39.120
But in this case,
we're going to talk

00:13:39.120 --> 00:13:42.600
about the entire environment
from the organization down

00:13:42.600 --> 00:13:45.450
and how easy it is to
automate the entire thing.

00:13:49.660 --> 00:13:54.740
So what problem are
we trying to solve?

00:13:54.740 --> 00:13:57.110
How many people here
are motivated to go

00:13:57.110 --> 00:14:03.840
to the cloud for the sake of the
agility that the cloud offers?

00:14:03.840 --> 00:14:06.460
I assume most everyone, right?

00:14:06.460 --> 00:14:08.850
And how many want to
achieve cost savings?

00:14:11.590 --> 00:14:14.530
Well, we want them
both, don't we?

00:14:14.530 --> 00:14:15.100
Right?

00:14:15.100 --> 00:14:16.933
And that's the problem
we're trying to solve

00:14:16.933 --> 00:14:19.930
is to drive the highest
value out of Cloud computing.

00:14:19.930 --> 00:14:23.530
But it's challenging
in that most of us

00:14:23.530 --> 00:14:25.900
are coming from a
static environment

00:14:25.900 --> 00:14:27.820
and going to a dynamic one.

00:14:27.820 --> 00:14:29.740
And while that
dynamic environment

00:14:29.740 --> 00:14:33.100
gives us the agility
we're seeking,

00:14:33.100 --> 00:14:35.510
it also raises new concerns.

00:14:35.510 --> 00:14:39.850
Things like security, how do you
support fast-paced environment

00:14:39.850 --> 00:14:43.570
like that, and also how
do you apply cost controls

00:14:43.570 --> 00:14:46.160
into that environment?

00:14:46.160 --> 00:14:48.310
So what is the solution to that?

00:14:48.310 --> 00:14:52.420
We have to balance
all those things--

00:14:52.420 --> 00:14:54.400
the need for the
controls, but also

00:14:54.400 --> 00:14:57.670
to enable our cloud
development teams

00:14:57.670 --> 00:15:00.460
to do what they are there to
do, which is to create products,

00:15:00.460 --> 00:15:03.380
and innovate, and be agile.

00:15:03.380 --> 00:15:05.860
So what we did is we
gathered around and came up

00:15:05.860 --> 00:15:11.560
with some best practices or
principles around which we

00:15:11.560 --> 00:15:13.750
apply to everything
that we're doing.

00:15:13.750 --> 00:15:17.330
First of which is automate
everything, everything

00:15:17.330 --> 00:15:18.890
that we possibly can.

00:15:18.890 --> 00:15:24.420
But in that automation,
we also build in security,

00:15:24.420 --> 00:15:26.760
both in the foundation,
on the IaaS side,

00:15:26.760 --> 00:15:28.470
also in our pipelines.

00:15:28.470 --> 00:15:33.090
So we pursue the goal of
shifting left in our pipeline

00:15:33.090 --> 00:15:36.720
management to try
and test everything

00:15:36.720 --> 00:15:40.690
up in the build area, including
vulnerability testing.

00:15:40.690 --> 00:15:43.530
We also embrace
agility and change.

00:15:43.530 --> 00:15:45.270
We're moving from
an environment where

00:15:45.270 --> 00:15:49.350
we have a manual cab approval
mechanism to one where we

00:15:49.350 --> 00:15:50.970
want to have much more change--

00:15:50.970 --> 00:15:54.130
smaller changes but
many more changes.

00:15:54.130 --> 00:15:56.100
So we need to have the
automation in there

00:15:56.100 --> 00:16:00.940
as well and a way to track
the evidence of those changes.

00:16:00.940 --> 00:16:04.980
We also have a philosophy
in this best practice

00:16:04.980 --> 00:16:08.070
around, you build
it, you own it.

00:16:08.070 --> 00:16:11.470
And what's great about
that is, as we transform

00:16:11.470 --> 00:16:16.290
and our application teams gather
around that goal and that best

00:16:16.290 --> 00:16:19.350
practice, it gives
us the ability

00:16:19.350 --> 00:16:22.410
to be able to federate
out the support.

00:16:22.410 --> 00:16:24.980
So we birthright
monitoring, and logging,

00:16:24.980 --> 00:16:26.730
and we're able to
actually send the alerts

00:16:26.730 --> 00:16:29.520
straight back to the
application development team.

00:16:29.520 --> 00:16:33.000
They're able to resolve
incidents more quickly,

00:16:33.000 --> 00:16:36.000
increasing the availability
of the products and services

00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:36.600
that we have.

00:16:39.200 --> 00:16:42.400
So how do these best
practices manifest themselves

00:16:42.400 --> 00:16:46.770
in our CoreLogic
GCP environment?

00:16:46.770 --> 00:16:50.300
We started in July of last year.

00:16:50.300 --> 00:16:53.630
And we set out with
the first goal,

00:16:53.630 --> 00:16:58.370
to automate the environment
from the organization down.

00:16:58.370 --> 00:17:02.790
We produced a factory
for the network,

00:17:02.790 --> 00:17:05.150
which is a global network.

00:17:05.150 --> 00:17:08.480
Produced a factory for our
identity and access management

00:17:08.480 --> 00:17:11.220
permissions and privileges.

00:17:11.220 --> 00:17:15.510
We produced a factory for
managing firewall rules.

00:17:15.510 --> 00:17:18.780
And then we produced
the cornerstone

00:17:18.780 --> 00:17:23.470
of the entire environment,
which is the project factory.

00:17:23.470 --> 00:17:25.569
Now, the project
factory, which we're

00:17:25.569 --> 00:17:27.869
going to show some
code around that,

00:17:27.869 --> 00:17:30.240
has some input
points that we have

00:17:30.240 --> 00:17:32.490
to deliver in to create it.

00:17:32.490 --> 00:17:36.660
But the project factory
is the cornerstone.

00:17:36.660 --> 00:17:40.560
It provides a landing
zone that's hardened.

00:17:40.560 --> 00:17:43.350
That has a number
of features that we

00:17:43.350 --> 00:17:46.230
want to layer in for every
application team that's

00:17:46.230 --> 00:17:49.350
migrating up to GCP.

00:17:49.350 --> 00:17:51.970
So if you think about
this, we started in July.

00:17:51.970 --> 00:17:54.850
We automated this organization.

00:17:54.850 --> 00:18:00.090
We now have 600 projects
in the environment.

00:18:00.090 --> 00:18:02.830
It took us a couple of months to
get invest in that automation,

00:18:02.830 --> 00:18:03.330
right?

00:18:03.330 --> 00:18:09.720
But now we can change labels
and we can change configurations

00:18:09.720 --> 00:18:13.680
on projects across the entire
environment in five minutes.

00:18:13.680 --> 00:18:15.130
It's fantastic.

00:18:15.130 --> 00:18:17.890
It's the only way
we can really scale.

00:18:17.890 --> 00:18:24.710
Now, this next slide you've seen
before, but it is so important,

00:18:24.710 --> 00:18:26.690
and I wanted to
bring it up again.

00:18:26.690 --> 00:18:28.587
It is a process.

00:18:28.587 --> 00:18:30.170
And it's important
to really nail down

00:18:30.170 --> 00:18:33.980
this process for the
very sake of the outcomes

00:18:33.980 --> 00:18:36.200
that Ramsay indicated--

00:18:36.200 --> 00:18:40.370
speeding up your migration,
having repeatability.

00:18:40.370 --> 00:18:42.890
Having that cornerstone
of that project

00:18:42.890 --> 00:18:45.170
built in the
organization gives you

00:18:45.170 --> 00:18:49.860
consistency across the
entire environment.

00:18:49.860 --> 00:18:55.220
So we do things like create the
project, attach it to the VPC.

00:18:55.220 --> 00:18:57.740
These are all service
projects, by the way.

00:18:57.740 --> 00:18:59.510
We delete all the defaults.

00:18:59.510 --> 00:19:04.110
We set up the identity and
access management security.

00:19:04.110 --> 00:19:05.860
The next bullet is
really important.

00:19:05.860 --> 00:19:10.020
You have to assign that project
to the correct billing account,

00:19:10.020 --> 00:19:11.100
right?

00:19:11.100 --> 00:19:13.380
And then also set up
the usage data that

00:19:13.380 --> 00:19:15.820
corresponds to that project.

00:19:15.820 --> 00:19:18.900
And then we enable APIs
and we set our labels.

00:19:23.090 --> 00:19:26.540
So that's the project factory.

00:19:26.540 --> 00:19:31.230
Let's pivot to asset management.

00:19:31.230 --> 00:19:33.680
So we've always been
taught asset management is

00:19:33.680 --> 00:19:37.430
the number one thing we've
got to do in IT, right?

00:19:37.430 --> 00:19:43.080
So how does asset management
translate up into the cloud?

00:19:43.080 --> 00:19:44.640
It's a little bit different.

00:19:44.640 --> 00:19:47.180
You don't have this
static infrastructure.

00:19:47.180 --> 00:19:50.510
You have Cloud Services.

00:19:50.510 --> 00:19:52.910
You have ephemeral machines.

00:19:52.910 --> 00:19:56.270
How is it that you gain
the insights into what

00:19:56.270 --> 00:19:58.970
you have in that environment?

00:19:58.970 --> 00:20:02.660
Asset management's about
identifying, organizing,

00:20:02.660 --> 00:20:05.960
and allocating, and then
being able to take action

00:20:05.960 --> 00:20:08.100
on those assets.

00:20:08.100 --> 00:20:11.280
So what do we do?

00:20:11.280 --> 00:20:16.820
We wanted to define our
use cases in the cloud

00:20:16.820 --> 00:20:18.660
and around assets.

00:20:18.660 --> 00:20:22.220
So we came up with financial
use cases and operational use

00:20:22.220 --> 00:20:23.450
cases.

00:20:23.450 --> 00:20:26.220
And we defined those labels.

00:20:26.220 --> 00:20:30.720
And then we assign those
labels to the project

00:20:30.720 --> 00:20:33.120
at the project level
and resources as we

00:20:33.120 --> 00:20:34.400
deploy resources.

00:20:34.400 --> 00:20:37.070
Because that's all tied
to billing and usage data.

00:20:37.070 --> 00:20:39.360
It's the billing
and usage data that

00:20:39.360 --> 00:20:42.330
gives us the accuracy,
the precision of what's

00:20:42.330 --> 00:20:46.080
sitting in that cloud
environment down to the second.

00:20:46.080 --> 00:20:49.120
What else can do that?

00:20:49.120 --> 00:20:50.360
So that's what we do.

00:20:50.360 --> 00:20:51.540
We set all this up.

00:20:51.540 --> 00:20:53.460
And then when the
project gets built,

00:20:53.460 --> 00:20:55.980
we have that default
set of labels set up.

00:20:55.980 --> 00:21:00.160
So if a workload owner is
moving up into the cloud,

00:21:00.160 --> 00:21:02.670
there's already preset
labels there while they're

00:21:02.670 --> 00:21:05.340
working in sandbox
getting their automation,

00:21:05.340 --> 00:21:08.520
their pipeline set up,
so they can actually

00:21:08.520 --> 00:21:11.010
apply labels onto the
resources they're using

00:21:11.010 --> 00:21:12.790
and get down to a
more granular level.

00:21:12.790 --> 00:21:15.280
We have it at the project level.

00:21:15.280 --> 00:21:24.540
So we, in the labeling strategy,
produce these labels but.

00:21:24.540 --> 00:21:26.850
There's a few
requirements that everyone

00:21:26.850 --> 00:21:30.090
needs to be aware of
when going to the GCP.

00:21:30.090 --> 00:21:32.880
First of all, all labels
need to be lower case.

00:21:35.610 --> 00:21:38.080
They also can't have
special characters.

00:21:38.080 --> 00:21:41.250
So some of our use
cases require notifying

00:21:41.250 --> 00:21:43.600
the owners of those assets.

00:21:43.600 --> 00:21:46.990
We can't use special characters
like email addresses.

00:21:46.990 --> 00:21:51.210
So you have to have a
solution that allows for that.

00:21:51.210 --> 00:21:53.670
We actually strip out
those special characters

00:21:53.670 --> 00:21:55.260
and spell it out.

00:21:55.260 --> 00:21:58.410
And we're able to then translate
that back to the right email

00:21:58.410 --> 00:22:00.430
address.

00:22:00.430 --> 00:22:02.715
The other thing
to note is, as you

00:22:02.715 --> 00:22:07.290
are reporting on the
information coming out of GCP,

00:22:07.290 --> 00:22:11.340
is that BigQuery has all
the columns for the labels

00:22:11.340 --> 00:22:12.660
that you create.

00:22:12.660 --> 00:22:16.950
But services are running at a
different pace from each other

00:22:16.950 --> 00:22:20.310
and how they're populating
the data into those columns.

00:22:20.310 --> 00:22:24.630
So as some services mature,
they have more information

00:22:24.630 --> 00:22:27.512
being placed out into BigQuery.

00:22:27.512 --> 00:22:29.220
So you just need to
watch for that as you

00:22:29.220 --> 00:22:30.387
get into the reporting side.

00:22:32.990 --> 00:22:36.470
Our top 10 labels here--

00:22:36.470 --> 00:22:40.430
as I said, financial use cases,
and operational use cases.

00:22:40.430 --> 00:22:44.240
We actually have two sets
of company and cost codes,

00:22:44.240 --> 00:22:48.770
because on one hand, we want to
track the aggregate coming in

00:22:48.770 --> 00:22:52.370
from GCP from a billing
perspective, the discounts,

00:22:52.370 --> 00:22:53.020
and all that.

00:22:53.020 --> 00:22:55.820
But then the other hand, we
want to be able to allocate out

00:22:55.820 --> 00:22:59.530
to the workload owners.

00:22:59.530 --> 00:23:05.090
In the environment, we want to
track our migration workloads

00:23:05.090 --> 00:23:09.130
so we have an application
ecosystem label.

00:23:09.130 --> 00:23:14.555
We also track the environment,
sandbox, non prod, prod,

00:23:14.555 --> 00:23:15.055
the tier.

00:23:15.055 --> 00:23:18.630
There's web, app, data tier.

00:23:18.630 --> 00:23:22.720
Who the financial owner
is of those assets.

00:23:22.720 --> 00:23:24.540
Who the tech lead is.

00:23:24.540 --> 00:23:27.280
And then last but
not least is who

00:23:27.280 --> 00:23:30.490
is going to fix the
problems that come up

00:23:30.490 --> 00:23:33.550
when they are going to be
alerted from the monitoring,

00:23:33.550 --> 00:23:34.820
right?

00:23:34.820 --> 00:23:35.798
The resolver group.

00:23:39.542 --> 00:23:41.982
So I wanted to do
a little demo here.

00:23:41.982 --> 00:23:43.440
And what we're
going to do is we're

00:23:43.440 --> 00:23:46.280
going to talk about how to
create the common variables.

00:23:46.280 --> 00:23:48.440
We're going to go into
a null label module.

00:23:48.440 --> 00:23:53.710
And we're going then show the
project factory module as well.

00:23:53.710 --> 00:23:57.540
And Ramsey's going to help out
and take us to the source code.

00:24:00.190 --> 00:24:02.630
It does exist.

00:24:02.630 --> 00:24:07.870
So what you see here, you
see a number of modules.

00:24:07.870 --> 00:24:11.290
We have, as I was describing,
a firewall module.

00:24:11.290 --> 00:24:14.040
We have the iam module,
the nat, network.

00:24:14.040 --> 00:24:15.630
But the one we
want to begin with

00:24:15.630 --> 00:24:18.780
is the terraform
null label module.

00:24:18.780 --> 00:24:22.290
If you open that up, the first
thing that one needs to do

00:24:22.290 --> 00:24:28.180
is declare their variables
in the variable.tf file.

00:24:28.180 --> 00:24:31.550
How many people here are
really familiar with Terraform?

00:24:31.550 --> 00:24:32.160
Oh, beautiful.

00:24:32.160 --> 00:24:32.660
Right.

00:24:32.660 --> 00:24:34.952
So I'm telling you probably
something you already know.

00:24:34.952 --> 00:24:37.620
But you have to declare
your variables here.

00:24:37.620 --> 00:24:41.010
And as you can see, we
have our mandatory labels

00:24:41.010 --> 00:24:45.120
defined with the data types.

00:24:45.120 --> 00:24:46.780
Quite simple.

00:24:46.780 --> 00:24:52.230
Now, as we go into the main
file, just a couple of things

00:24:52.230 --> 00:24:52.980
I'm going to show.

00:24:52.980 --> 00:24:55.170
First thing, we do
have a reference

00:24:55.170 --> 00:24:58.680
here to our confluence page
so that anybody building out

00:24:58.680 --> 00:25:02.620
a null label file can
reference our standards,

00:25:02.620 --> 00:25:05.060
our operating standards.

00:25:05.060 --> 00:25:08.420
And then we have a
little bit of work

00:25:08.420 --> 00:25:13.980
here to do to make sure we
conform to GCP requirements.

00:25:13.980 --> 00:25:18.200
So we set a couple
regex expressions here

00:25:18.200 --> 00:25:21.830
to help clean up any
values that are coming

00:25:21.830 --> 00:25:24.440
in to the project factory.

00:25:24.440 --> 00:25:33.260
And we use the kebab case
to transform any capital

00:25:33.260 --> 00:25:37.180
letters to lowercase
and also to change

00:25:37.180 --> 00:25:40.250
any underscores to hyphens.

00:25:40.250 --> 00:25:43.850
And since you have a lot of
Terraform developers here,

00:25:43.850 --> 00:25:45.590
you know what the kebab case is.

00:25:45.590 --> 00:25:50.810
If not, it is a real thing,
just like the snake case,

00:25:50.810 --> 00:25:52.280
and the camel case.

00:25:52.280 --> 00:25:54.050
Well, we use it here.

00:25:54.050 --> 00:25:55.880
And the whole point
is that we can

00:25:55.880 --> 00:25:59.720
compartmentalize these variables
into a separate module.

00:25:59.720 --> 00:26:02.460
We can have some of this
pre processing like this.

00:26:02.460 --> 00:26:04.880
We can clean up the
values so that we

00:26:04.880 --> 00:26:09.280
conform to GCP requirements.

00:26:09.280 --> 00:26:13.310
Now we also reference, in
these kebab case statements,

00:26:13.310 --> 00:26:15.800
every one of the
mandatory labels, right?

00:26:15.800 --> 00:26:17.420
And once we're
done with that, we

00:26:17.420 --> 00:26:19.550
can go to the project
factory module.

00:26:26.480 --> 00:26:28.580
And here's the thing to note.

00:26:28.580 --> 00:26:31.010
The very first thing is
that we want to reference

00:26:31.010 --> 00:26:33.380
that null label module.

00:26:33.380 --> 00:26:33.880
Right?

00:26:33.880 --> 00:26:41.150
And then reference
the variables that

00:26:41.150 --> 00:26:44.786
are going to be used
in this main tf file.

00:26:44.786 --> 00:26:48.890
So we have here
reference all 10 of

00:26:48.890 --> 00:26:51.380
the CoreLogic mandatory labels.

00:26:51.380 --> 00:26:54.050
And then as we
scroll down, we also

00:26:54.050 --> 00:26:56.300
have information on
the project creation

00:26:56.300 --> 00:27:01.850
that needs to be populated--
the project ID, the organization

00:27:01.850 --> 00:27:05.650
ID, the folder ID.

00:27:05.650 --> 00:27:09.870
And it's vital that you
add the billing account.

00:27:09.870 --> 00:27:13.540
And then we also have the
network that we attached to.

00:27:13.540 --> 00:27:16.600
And then we reference
the label module

00:27:16.600 --> 00:27:23.170
again here so that
when you run the apply,

00:27:23.170 --> 00:27:27.650
it's going to pull labels
from the right place, right?

00:27:27.650 --> 00:27:29.310
OK.

00:27:29.310 --> 00:27:33.660
So our engineers created
this and they set up

00:27:33.660 --> 00:27:38.250
a little example for us where
we just hard code the values

00:27:38.250 --> 00:27:41.250
into the label variables.

00:27:41.250 --> 00:27:46.950
And you can see here the hard
coded values going into the 10

00:27:46.950 --> 00:27:48.550
labels that we have.

00:27:48.550 --> 00:27:53.580
Now, this is in the main tf file
that would be in the project

00:27:53.580 --> 00:27:55.780
factory module.

00:27:55.780 --> 00:27:58.230
But we don't do it this way.

00:27:58.230 --> 00:28:01.990
We use the .tfvar file
as part of our pipeline.

00:28:01.990 --> 00:28:05.070
So we can add a form
in front and automate

00:28:05.070 --> 00:28:06.450
the entire process.

00:28:06.450 --> 00:28:10.080
And we do that for every
one of our modules.

00:28:10.080 --> 00:28:15.980
So takeaways from this is
modularize your Terraform code.

00:28:15.980 --> 00:28:19.850
Leverage the modules to be
able to separate your variables

00:28:19.850 --> 00:28:22.600
and variable processing
from your main code.

00:28:22.600 --> 00:28:25.100
And set up a proper
structure that can scale.

00:28:27.880 --> 00:28:28.590
OK.

00:28:28.590 --> 00:28:34.110
So now you can run your plan.

00:28:34.110 --> 00:28:37.830
And then run your apply.

00:28:37.830 --> 00:28:40.680
And with that, then
Ramsay, I think

00:28:40.680 --> 00:28:43.770
we would go to the
GUI in the GCP Console

00:28:43.770 --> 00:28:45.065
to take a look at that.

00:28:45.065 --> 00:28:46.190
RAMSEY HADDAD: That's good.

00:28:46.190 --> 00:28:48.240
Yeah, we can flip over
to the slides, please.

00:28:56.240 --> 00:29:00.520
JIM WOLCOTT: So everyone's
familiar with the GCP Console.

00:29:00.520 --> 00:29:03.432
You look under the iam in the
admin section, and of course,

00:29:03.432 --> 00:29:05.140
go to labels and see
the key value pairs.

00:29:07.760 --> 00:29:09.035
Just that simple.

00:29:09.035 --> 00:29:13.620
A couple of sample reports we
just put together out of this.

00:29:13.620 --> 00:29:17.260
Number one, we want to look at
the aggregate by cost center.

00:29:17.260 --> 00:29:19.670
And we have a lot
of projects that

00:29:19.670 --> 00:29:23.080
might group up under one primary
cost center or a chargeback

00:29:23.080 --> 00:29:23.580
cost center.

00:29:23.580 --> 00:29:26.555
So you'll see how those
cost centers are trending.

00:29:29.280 --> 00:29:32.690
Second is in your
migrations, you're

00:29:32.690 --> 00:29:35.360
going to want to track
your work packages.

00:29:35.360 --> 00:29:38.630
And so having that
ecosystem ID to be

00:29:38.630 --> 00:29:41.100
able to identify the
package is really

00:29:41.100 --> 00:29:43.530
helpful in a number of ways.

00:29:43.530 --> 00:29:46.770
You can see how that package
is moving into your sandbox,

00:29:46.770 --> 00:29:49.770
into your non prod
environments, and into prod.

00:29:49.770 --> 00:29:53.160
Also can see the effect
of any of your packages

00:29:53.160 --> 00:29:56.190
on the shared services
projects, such as network

00:29:56.190 --> 00:29:57.510
or your security projects.

00:29:57.510 --> 00:30:01.170
And you start getting
insights into what is

00:30:01.170 --> 00:30:04.950
happening in your migrations.

00:30:04.950 --> 00:30:05.450
Great.

00:30:05.450 --> 00:30:07.220
And so with that, thank you.

00:30:07.220 --> 00:30:08.445
I'll hand it back to Ramsey.

00:30:08.445 --> 00:30:09.570
RAMSEY HADDAD: Thanks, Jim.

00:30:13.500 --> 00:30:16.710
So I wanted just to summarize
some of the key takeaways

00:30:16.710 --> 00:30:17.460
from this session.

00:30:17.460 --> 00:30:20.970
I know we went over
this relatively quickly.

00:30:20.970 --> 00:30:24.660
But it's really important that
you set these best practices

00:30:24.660 --> 00:30:25.230
up early.

00:30:25.230 --> 00:30:28.350
As you kind of saw Jim
describe, CoreLogic

00:30:28.350 --> 00:30:30.330
had these set of
10 required labels.

00:30:30.330 --> 00:30:33.060
And the exercises that
we actually stepped them

00:30:33.060 --> 00:30:35.532
through early on was actually
to go through and build

00:30:35.532 --> 00:30:37.740
that naming convention,
build that key and value pair

00:30:37.740 --> 00:30:38.627
combination.

00:30:38.627 --> 00:30:40.710
That was not only relevant
to the business owners,

00:30:40.710 --> 00:30:42.877
but that also made it really
easy for the developers

00:30:42.877 --> 00:30:46.890
to interpret and incorporate
that into their pipeline.

00:30:46.890 --> 00:30:50.250
Automating your project build--
so this notion of project

00:30:50.250 --> 00:30:53.400
factory is an excellent place to
actually put a lot of your best

00:30:53.400 --> 00:30:55.450
practices in control.

00:30:55.450 --> 00:30:57.120
So really look to
the project creation

00:30:57.120 --> 00:31:02.140
to ingest a lot of those
best practices as possible.

00:31:02.140 --> 00:31:05.560
Using billing labels-- so one of
the things that we commonly see

00:31:05.560 --> 00:31:09.100
is that either the labels
aren't being attached

00:31:09.100 --> 00:31:13.150
in an appropriate way or they're
inconsistent and actually

00:31:13.150 --> 00:31:15.190
aren't structured in
a uniform way that

00:31:15.190 --> 00:31:16.700
makes sense to the business.

00:31:16.700 --> 00:31:18.820
So we want to encourage
you to use billing labels.

00:31:18.820 --> 00:31:21.400
But also make sure that you
incorporate those labels

00:31:21.400 --> 00:31:23.540
into your infrastructure
as code pipeline.

00:31:23.540 --> 00:31:26.050
So take all of these
costs governance,

00:31:26.050 --> 00:31:28.883
your billing approach, your
billing account association.

00:31:28.883 --> 00:31:30.550
And then take that
and then really start

00:31:30.550 --> 00:31:32.917
to apply labels against that.

00:31:32.917 --> 00:31:35.500
And then make sure that you have
that incorporated in your CSE

00:31:35.500 --> 00:31:38.280
pipeline.

00:31:38.280 --> 00:31:40.480
A few resources
to leave you with.

00:31:40.480 --> 00:31:43.020
So there's a project internal
to Google called the Cloud

00:31:43.020 --> 00:31:45.855
Foundation Toolkit,
where a group of Googlers

00:31:45.855 --> 00:31:48.600
have actually come together and
built a standard set of modules

00:31:48.600 --> 00:31:51.300
in both Terraform and
Deployment Manager.

00:31:51.300 --> 00:31:53.910
For things to solve such
as project creation,

00:31:53.910 --> 00:31:56.880
associating labels, all
the way up the stack

00:31:56.880 --> 00:32:00.960
into things such as container
deployments and cloud storage,

00:32:00.960 --> 00:32:02.908
as well.

00:32:02.908 --> 00:32:04.950
Some resources if you're
interested in leveraging

00:32:04.950 --> 00:32:05.965
these modules.

00:32:05.965 --> 00:32:08.340
You can actually head over to
github.com/terraform google

00:32:08.340 --> 00:32:11.148
modules.

00:32:11.148 --> 00:32:12.690
And there's an active
community, as I

00:32:12.690 --> 00:32:15.570
mentioned, through the
Terraform provider for GCP.

00:32:15.570 --> 00:32:18.060
There's also an active community
within Google building up

00:32:18.060 --> 00:32:20.290
those modules that fit
enterprise use cases.

